gm-method/rendering (an instanced cluster-LOD model shows 'parts appear/disappear as the camera moves around it', most visible on FLAT/THIN pieces): the per-cluster frustum cull tests a bounding SPHERE per cluster, and a sphere UNDER-COVERS a thin flat slab (a ~0.1-0.2m-thick floor/wall/panel cluster has a small, centred sphere). At a close camera the small sphere can fall just outside the frustum while the slab's broad face is still on-screen -> the slab false-culls; rotating the camera flips different thin slabs in/out = the flicker. DECISIVE WITNESS: at a close/live camera, for each cluster compute its TRUE geometry AABB (from the actual LOD0 vertices, not the baked sphere) and test AABB-in-frustum-but-not-drawn -> the wrongly-culled set, and confirm the missing pieces are all THIN slabs (one axis ~0.1-0.2m). BEWARE two measurement traps that wasted effort: (1) comparing the lib's drawn count to the SAME baked sphere is a tautology (always agrees) -- you must test against independent geometry; (2) a scripted orbit camera at model-scale distance puts the whole model in-frustum so NOTHING culls (429/429) and hides the bug -- reproduce at the LIVE close camera. FIX for an always-near hero ENVIRONMENT model: draw every cluster (disable the per-cluster frustum cull) -- it is one small always-near object so the CPU cull saves nothing, and this matches the always-near 'frustumCulled=false' idiom; the per-cluster projected-size LOD selection still runs. A sphere-WIDENING fix (recompute sphere = true-AABB) is tempting but noisy (it also over-draws and the LOD-feeding radius shifts); disabling the cull outright is the clean, provable form (drawn==total at every angle). Implement as a consumer flag on the cluster mesh (set in the ModelPool ready callback) honored by a marker-gated lib patch that skips the frustum `continue`; patch the dist copy too for the static deploy.